=\left(25+\ln 5\right) - (1+ \ln 1) = 24+\ln 5

statement.p:
Find the curvature of the plane curve
y=-t^{2}
at the point t=1.

static.solution:
\kappa(t)=\frac{||\mathbf{r}'(t)\times \mathbf{r}''(t)||}{||\mathbf{r}'(t)||^3}.
We now find
\mathbf{r}'(t) = \langle -4 t^{-2}, 0, 1 \rangle
then
\mathbf{r}''(t) = \langle 8 t^{-3}, 0, 0 \rangle
and so their cross product is
\begin{array}{rcl}
\mathbf{r}'(t)\times \mathbf{r}''(t)
\amp  = \amp  \left|\begin{array}{ccc}\mathbf{i} \amp  \mathbf{j} \amp  \mathbf{k}\\ -4 t^{-2} \amp  0 \amp  1 \\ 8 t^{-3} \amp  0 \amp  0\end{array}\right| \\
\amp  = \amp 
 \left|\begin{array}{cc}0 \amp  1 \\ 0 \amp  0\end{array}\right|\mathbf{i}
-\left|\begin{array}{cc}-4 t^{-2} \amp  1 \\ 8 t^{-3} \amp  0\end{array}\right|\mathbf{j}
+\left|\begin{array}{cc}-4 t^{-2} \amp  0 \\ 8 t^{-3} \amp  0\end{array}\right|\mathbf{k}
 \\
\amp  = \amp  \langle 0, 8 t^{-3}, 0\rangle.
\end{array}
We compute the necessary lengths:
\begin{array}{rcl}
||\mathbf{r}'(t)\times \mathbf{r}''(t)|| \amp  = \amp  8 |t|^{-3} \\
||\mathbf{r}'(t)|| \amp  = \amp  \sqrt{(-4 t^{-2})^2+0^2+1^2}=\sqrt{16 t^{-4}+1}
\end{array}
leaving
\kappa(t)=\frac{8 |t|^{-3}}{(16 t^{-4}+1)^{3/2}}
which can be simplified to
\frac{8 |t|^3}{(16+1 t^4)^{3/2}}.

t, obtaining y = x^{2/3}. This gives us y as a function of x, and we notice that y is always positive, so the correct graph is C.
We calculate the velocity and acceleration by taking derivatives: \begin{array}{rcl} \mathbf{v}(t) \amp =\amp \left\lt 3t^{2},2t\right> \\ \mathbf{a}(t) \amp =\amp \left\lt 6t,2\right> \end{array} and then plug in t=1 to obtain \begin{array}{rcl} \mathbf{v}(1) \amp =\amp \left\lt 3,2\right> \\ \mathbf{a}(1) \amp =\amp \left\lt 6,2\right> \end{array}
It remains to determine the direction of motion. Since the graph is y as a function of x, we know the direction is either "Left-to-Right" or "Right-to-Left." Since \frac{d x}{dt}=3t^{2} is always positive, x is always increasing and we determine that the direction is "Left-to-Right".

