  $fig[$i] = image(insertGraph($gr[$i]),width=>"400",height=>"400",tex_size=>"700");
}



########################################
#  Main text

BEGIN_TEXT
Suppose \[ \vec{F}(x,y) = $F \] and \( C \) is the 
counter-clockwise oriented sector of a circle centered
at the origin with radius \( $r \) and central angle
\( \pi / $d \).
Use Green's theorem to calculate the 
circulation of \( \vec{F} \) around \( C \).
$BR
$BR
Circulation = 
\{ans_rule(40)\}
$BR
$fig[0]
$BR
(Click on graph to enlarge)
END_TEXT


###############################################
#  Answer evaluation

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;

ANS($answer->cmp() );

$cb = $c + $b;
Context("Fraction");
$aa = Compute("$cb*($r^2)/(2*$d)");
Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_SOLUTION
$PAR SOLUTION $BR $BR
Let \(D\) be the region enclosed by the curve \(C\). $BR
First note that the curve has a positive orientation. We will use Green's Theorem.
We have \( P = $a x - $b y, \; Q = $c x \), therefore
\[ \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} =$c+ $b = $cb .\]
Hence, Green's Theorem implies $BR
\( \begin{array}{lcl} \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot  d\vec{r}& = & 
\iint_D \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) dA
\\ &=& \iint_D $cb dA  
\\ & = & $cb \int_0^{\pi/$d} \int_0^{$r} r \, dr \, d\theta \quad \text{[Using polar coordinates]}
\\& = & $cb \left(\frac{\pi}{$d}\right) \left(\frac{ $r^2}{2}\right)
\\&=& $aa \pi
\end{array}\)
END_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

li.p:
If S_1 is a rectangle with area 1 and S_2
is a rectangle with area 2, then 
\displaystyle 2 \iint_{S_1} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} 
= \iint_{S_2} \vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A}

statement.p:
Use the divergence theorem to calculate the flux of 
the vector field \vec{F}(x,y,z) = x^3 \vec{i}
+ y^3 \vec{j} + z^3 \vec{k}
out of the closed, outward-oriented surface S 
bounding the solid x^2 + y^2 \leq 4, \ 
0 \leq z \leq 6.

static.solution:
\mbox{div}\,{\vec{F}} = 4x + 2y + 4z. So, if W is the interior of the box, we have \int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} = \int_{W}(4x + 2y + 4z)dV = \int_{1}^{4}\int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{3}(4x + 2y + 4z)dz\,dy\,dx. Integrating through each of the integrals in turn, we have \int_{S}\vec{F} \cdot d\vec{A} = \int_{1}^{4}\int_{0}^{3} 4x\,z + 2y\,z + 2z^{2}\bigg|_{z=0}^{z=3}\, dy\,dx = \int_{1}^{4}\int_{0}^{3} 12 x + 6 y + 18\,dy\,dx = \int_{1}^{4} 12 x y + 3 y^2 + 18 y\bigg|_{y=0}^{y=3}\,dx = \int_{1}^{4} 36 x + 27 + 54\, dx = 18 x^2 + 27 x + 54 x\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=4} = 513.
Finding flux directly, note that on the faces x=4 and x=1 the normal vectors to the surfaces are \vec i and -\vec i respectively, so that \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 2x^{2}\,dy\,dz (where x is evaluated at 4 or 1), and similarly on y = 3 and y = 0, and z = 3 and z = 0, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm y^{2}\,dx\,dz and \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \pm 2z^{2}\,dx\,dy, respectively. Thus on y = 0 and z = 0 the flux is zero. On the remaining faces, we have
(1) On x = 4, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{3}\,32\,dz\,dy = 288;
(2) On x = 1, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{0}^{3}\int_{0}^{3}\,2\,dz\,dy = -18;
(3) On y = 3, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{1}^{4}\int_{0}^{3}\,9\,dz\,dx = 81; and
(4) On z = 3, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = \int_{1}^{4}\int_{0}^{3}\,18\,dy\,dx = 162. Thus the total flux is the sum of these, \vec F\cdot d\vec A = 513.

x-axis rather than on the y-axis and the directrix is a vertical line rather than horizontal as in the parabola in standard position.
Therefore, the equation of the parabola is obtained by interchanging x and y in y=\frac{1}{4c}{x}^{2}.
Also, by the given information c=5. Hence, x=\frac{1}{4c}{y}^{2}=\frac{1}{4\cdot 5}{y}^{2} or x=\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}

